it plays SNES games it's portable it  connects to a TV sound familiar the  hyper can Superboy SFC is essentially a  Nintendo switch from the 90s it's a  novel and streamlined way to play your  original NTSC and PAL SNES cartridges  anywhere but it does have shortcomings  which make this hulking handheld a less  than ideal way to play your old carts  this is an updated version of hyper  conceived eeeh Superboy s from back in  2012 but now we have a few new features  and the Super Famicom color scheme the  Superboy SFC is available now for a  hundred US dollars now let's get into  some of the technical details the  Superboy weighs a little over 2 pounds  and measures in and about 11 inches wide  eight inches tall with a cart loaded and  three inches deep compared to the  Nintendo switch this thing's a bit thick  it's also due to the fact that you need  to load physical cards on top of the  device pin perfect technology helps card  stay in place and keep games running  even if their device gets jostled around  however it's sometimes difficult to  wedge cartridges in there without  feeling like you're gonna break  something this is especially true for  Super Famicom carts since the casing  doesn't sit perfectly flush I was  reluctant to force a Japanese Super  Mario World cartridge in there but I got  it to work just be very careful the  controller layout in organics are fairly  decent considering the size and design  face buttons shoulder buttons and the  d-pad feel good to press if you have  smaller hands it might be a bit tough to  get them around the device though two  front-facing speakers provide adequate  audio quality despite a lack in bass the  wonderful soundtrack of Chrono Trigger  doesn't sound tinny you can also use  headphones or earbuds with a 3.5  millimeter port but a light buzz can be  heard in quieter moments another feature  to SNES controller ports for multiplayer  games and better TV playability if you  have the old game pads battery life is  quite impressive at about 10 hours from  a full charge I used it for about 5 to 6  hours in total and didn't have to charge  once using 75% brightness when you do  have to charge a superboy it's done  through a micro USB port on top here you  can also find the pal ntsc switch the  reset button the power switch and AV out  port along the bottom is a 3.5  millimeter audio jack the volume wheel  and brightness aspect ratio combo button  for brightness levels are offered and  the max level is good enough to you  outdoors if you hold that down for about  three seconds it changes the aspect  ratio now the Superboy SFC sports a 4.3  inch LCD screen displays both  traditional 4 by 3 aspect ratio and 16  by 9 widescreen I don't find the 16 by 9  particularly useful as it just stretches  the normal image it feels like wasted  screen space on top of that the actual  image quality isn't that great because  of its washed out colors and fuzzy  motion these issues are exacerbated when  hooking it up to a TV it's a real neat  feature throwing your SNES games onto  the big screen seamlessly like a switch  seems dope but the amount of ghosting  you get is a deal-breaker for any game  that moves somewhat fast like Super  Mario World even in a turn-based RPG  like Chrono Trigger navigating the world  just doesn't look right when the image  looks so fuzzy in motion you should note  that the super boy only connects to an  external display through the package  composite cables plenty of modern TVs  still have those red white and yellow  inputs but it limits the visual quality  input lag is also something to consider  if you're gonna play on the TV  since the Superboy display still runs  while connected to a TV you can  immediately see a side-by-side of the  noticeable input delay when jumping  between the analog super Antti and the  Superboy it's clear how high perkins  offering isn't the best way to play  these games due to shortcomings in both  response time and color reproduction  despite all the compromises made in the  Superboy SFC there was something special  about playing these old cartridges on a  handheld sure it's bulky and I might be  awkward to have large and probably  valuable carts sticking out of the back  of a portable device but I had a ton of  fun kicking my feet up on the couch and  giving an earnest playthrough of some of  my favorite SNES games just don't expect  the highest quality visuals or a viable  way to play on TVs  [Music]
 

